+- 2026-10-24 (2): live atmosphere editor (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +2 tests in `world::atmos`, +1 in `options.rs`: `cargo test --lib atmos options`).
+    - `world::atmos`: the table is data now. `AtmosCfg` (in `Settings::atmos`) holds the 19 looks (`of` gives the defaults), `here` (the biome under the player, written by `lib.rs` each frame) and `view` (the editor's preview, never saved). `Atmos::update(dt, at, &cfg, biome)` reads the looks from it and returns the player's biome; with `view` set every column counts as that biome and the frame reaches it in one step, so a slider is seen in the next frame; without it nothing changed (9 columns, 2 s). `TABS` / `Knob` describe the editor's sliders (slot, range, how it reads), `name` / `ALL` / `idx` the biomes.
+    - `options.rs`: `OptScr::Atmos`, reached from Video Settings by a 13 x 13 pixel sun (`sun`, drawn from quads) in the bottom right corner, only when `OptionsUi::in_game()` (the pause menu; the title screen has no world to show). `atmos_widgets` lays out the panel (212 GUI units wide, 240 high at the least); no dimming, only a shade under the panel. Sliders are the existing `Act` sliders (`AtKnob`), saved when let go; Done ends the preview (`view = None`; `lib.rs` also clears it whenever the game is not paused) and the sky eases back to the biome under the player.
+    - `lib.rs`: the paused idle poll (90 ms) is off while `view` is set, so the clouds keep drifting behind the editor.
+    - Not done: previewing another time of day or weather (the colours edited are the noon ones; night and rain follow the vanilla curves), copy / paste of a look, the Nether (the editor opens there but there is nothing to see: its sky is `Frame::OFF`).
 - 2026-10-24: biome atmosphere: a unique sky, fog, haze, clouds and stars per biome, eased in real time (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +5 tests in `world::atmos`, +1 in `world::sky`; run `cargo test --lib atmos sky` first, then look at it).
     - `world/atmos.rs` (new, pure): `of(Biome)` = the look of each of the 19 biomes (the table to tune: day colours of the sky top, horizon = fog, haze band, exp² fog density, cloud tint / cover / speed, star tint / brightness, gradient exponent). `Atmos::update` averages the looks of 9 columns (centre + a ring of 48 blocks) four times a second and chases that goal with `TAU` = 2 s in real seconds (`1 - exp(-dt / TAU)`: frame rate independent); `reset` makes the next update jump (new world, Nether). `Atmos::frame(angle, rain, thunder)` gives the renderers a `Frame`. The vanilla colours (`world::sky`) are kept and multiplied by `biome noon colour / vanilla noon colour`, so at noon the sky is exactly the table's colour, night stays dark, rain and thunder still grey it. The temperature term of the old sky colour is gone (the biome decides the hue); `ChunkManager::temperature_at` is deleted.
     - Fog: `fogp.z` = the biome's density; the chunk / LoD shaders (WGSL + GLSL) leave `(1 - linear) * exp(-(d z)^2)` of a thing d blocks away, so a swamp is thick at 100 blocks and a mountain is clear, and the LoD's linear fog still hides its edge. Rain raises it x2.5.
